-          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, $G:U\\to\\mathbb R^m$ be $C^1$, $z_0\\in U$, residual tolerance $\\varepsilon\\ge0$, correction budget $N\\in\\mathbb N_0$, and refresh period $q\\in\\mathbb N$, $q\\ge1$. Use the Jacobian convention $J(z)=DG(z)$ of [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]. The output is a last valid iterate $z\\in U$, a status, and the actual residual norm $\\|G(z)\\|_2$.\n\nIn **updated-Newton mode**, invoke [[alg-finite-budget-newton-for-vector-residuals]] with $G,z_0,\\varepsilon,N$. It recomputes the Jacobian at every correction and returns its stated residual/budget/failure status. Re-evaluate $G$ at the returned iterate to provide the output residual norm. Count each Jacobian assembly, factorization attempt, and linear solve actually performed.\n\nIn **chord mode**, for $k=0,1,\\ldots,N$, first evaluate $G(z_k)$ and put $r_k=\\|G(z_k)\\|_2$. If $r_k\\le\\varepsilon$, return $(z_k,\\texttt{root},r_k)$; this status means only that the residual tolerance was met. If $k=N$, return $(z_k,\\texttt{iteration-budget-exhausted},r_k)$. Otherwise, at $k=0,q,2q,\\ldots$, refresh by forming $J_k=DG(z_k)$, attempting to factor it, and retaining the factorization until the next scheduled refresh. The factorization backend must either report singularity or return a factor object whose solve operation gives the exact unique solution of $J_k s=-G(z_k)$. If it reports singularity, return $(z_k,\\texttt{singular-jacobian},r_k)$. At each correction call [[alg-newton-correction-for-vector-residuals]] with the retained $J_k$ and current residual. If that procedure reports a singular Jacobian, return $(z_k,\\texttt{singular-jacobian},r_k)$. If its correction $s_k$ has $z_k+s_k\\notin U$, return $(z_k,\\texttt{invalid-trial},r_k)$; otherwise set $z_{k+1}=z_k+s_k$ and continue. A refresh period $q=1$ updates the Jacobian every correction; $q>1$ is a chord iteration.\n\nBoth modes make no claim that a finite budget reaches an exact root or that a small residual is a forward-error bound. Record the number of Jacobian assemblies, factorization attempts, and linear solves actually performed; a dense LU correction uses one forward and one backward triangular solve. Sparse cost depends on the actual stage matrix and factorization backend and is not bounded here.",
+          "quote": "Let $m\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, and let $G:U\\to\\mathbb R^m$ be $C^1$, $z_0\\in U$, residual tolerance $\\varepsilon\\ge0$, correction budget $N\\in\\mathbb N_0$, and refresh period $q\\in\\mathbb N$, $q\\ge1$. Use the coordinate Jacobian convention of [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]. Since $G$ is $C^1$, [[thm-total-derivative-computes-directional-and-partial-derivatives]] identifies this Jacobian with the matrix of $DG(z)$; write it as $J(z)$. Residual and Jacobian evaluations, norm comparisons and domain tests are returning primitives; no bound on their internal cost is specified. In chord mode also supply a terminating exact linear-system backend: on each square Jacobian it reports singularity if the matrix is singular, and otherwise returns a retained factor object, and each solve on that object terminates with the exact unique solution for its right-hand side. Factorization and solve work are charged individually. The output is a last valid iterate $z\\in U$, a status, and the actual residual norm $\\|G(z)\\|_2$.\n\nIn **updated-Newton mode**, invoke [[alg-finite-budget-newton-for-vector-residuals]] with $G,z_0,\\varepsilon,N$. It recomputes the Jacobian at every correction and returns its stated residual/budget/failure status. Re-evaluate $G$ at the returned iterate to provide the output residual norm. Count each Jacobian assembly, factorization attempt, and linear solve actually performed.\n\nIn **chord mode**, for $k=0,1,\\ldots,N$, first evaluate $G(z_k)$ and put $r_k=\\|G(z_k)\\|_2$. If $r_k\\le\\varepsilon$, return $(z_k,\\texttt{root},r_k)$; this status means only that the residual tolerance was met. If $k=N$, return $(z_k,\\texttt{iteration-budget-exhausted},r_k)$. Otherwise, at $k=0,q,2q,\\ldots$, refresh by forming $J_k=DG(z_k)$, attempting to factor it, and retaining the factorization until the next scheduled refresh. The factorization backend must either report singularity or return a factor object whose solve operation gives the exact unique solution of $J_k s=-G(z_k)$. If it reports singularity, return $(z_k,\\texttt{singular-jacobian},r_k)$. At each correction obtain $s_k$ by calling the solve operation on that retained factor object with right-hand side $-G(z_k)$; do not refactor or invoke a fresh matrix-only solve. The exact-solve backend contract makes this the mathematical correction in [[alg-newton-correction-for-vector-residuals]]. If the correction $s_k$ has $z_k+s_k\\notin U$, return $(z_k,\\texttt{invalid-trial},r_k)$; otherwise set $z_{k+1}=z_k+s_k$ and continue. A refresh period $q=1$ updates the Jacobian every correction; $q>1$ is a chord iteration.\n\nBoth modes make no claim that a finite budget reaches an exact root or that a small residual is a forward-error bound. Record the number of Jacobian assemblies, factorization attempts, and linear solves actually performed; a dense LU correction uses one forward and one backward triangular solve. Sparse cost depends on the actual stage matrix and factorization backend and is not bounded here.",
