se solve operation gives the exact unique solution of $J_k s=-G(z_k)$. If it reports singularity, return $(z_k,\\texttt{singular-jacobian},r_k)$. At each correction obtain $s_k$ by calling the solve operation on that retained factor object with right-hand side $-G(z_k)$; do not refactor or invoke a fresh matrix-only solve. The exact-solve backend contract makes this the mathematical correction in [[alg-newton-correction-for-vector-residuals]]. If the correction $s_k$ has $z_k+s_k\\notin U$, return $(z_k,\\texttt{invalid-trial},r_k)$; otherwise set $z_{k+1}=z_k+s_k$ and continue. A refresh period $q=1$ updates the Jacobian every correction; $q>1$ is a chord iteration.\n\nBoth modes make no claim that a finite budget reaches an exact root or that a small residual is a forward-error bound. Record the number of Jacobian assemblies, factorization attempts, and linear solves actually performed; a dense LU correction uses one forward and one backward triangular solve. Sparse cost depends on the actual stage matrix and factorization backend and is not bounded here.",
